L wrote:
> >False.  Two of the four uses you listed on your post are significant:
> >shop prices and succubus
>
> Both of which are _extremely specific_ and do not justify adding an
> entire stat just for them.

They are not extremely specific: shops are a major part of the game,
and all players encounter succubuses and incubuses at times.  Just
because something is only used in a small number of spots in the source
code does not mean those spots are not significant.

I where we part ways is in the realization that this specificness is in
the nature of the concept of Charisma, and many roleplaying games
struggle with giving it a good-enough role in the game.  Charisma is in
Nethack because it is in Dungeons & Dragons, it's one of those things
that ties the game back to pencil-and-paper roots, like armor classes
that count down and demon princes.  While I don't think all patch
authors work on their modifications with the hope that they someday be
adopted by the Dev Team, if this is your hope then I would suggest
finding new uses for Charisma rather than removing it, as I don't think
the Dev Team would take it out by now.


> I mean, the second-to-least used statistic, intelligence, may only
> really influence spellcasting (as well as some specialities), but at
> least spellcasting is diverse enough for high intelligence to be
> worthwhile to spell-casting players at almost any stage of the game.
> Meanwhile, shop prices become irrelevant halfway through the midgame -
> often sooner.

Again, I think you should be finding new uses for Charisma.  It seems
like you're removing it more to make some kind of philosophical point
than improve the game.


On the other hand, I don't think maintaining save compatibility is a
virtue.  There's nothing stopping players from keeping a copy of
vanilla on-hand to play those games with.

